From d880d525f47eb2a7b561fa35e979cb934454ee90 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 19:09:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 20/20] Implement wp_presentation

Vendor the stable presentation-time protocol and advertise
wp_presentation at version 1; mpv uses it to estimate display
timing. The clock is CLOCK_MONOTONIC where the system has
clock_gettime (Mac OS X 10.12+, MacPorts legacy-support on older
systems, GNUstep), with a gettimeofday/CLOCK_REALTIME fallback
otherwise.

Feedback objects ride along the frame-callback machinery: collected
into the surface's pending state, moved to the surface on commit,
and completed with presented from -drawRect: (or straight from
commit when no redraw is needed, as the content on screen is
already identical). Unlike frame callbacks, they complete with
discarded when their content update never reaches the screen: on a
commit that supersedes a still-undrawn one, on an unmapping commit,
and on surface destruction. presented and discarded are destructor
events, so the feedback resource is destroyed after sending, and
each feedback survives its resource dying first on an abrupt client
disconnect by turning both sends into no-ops.

The presented arguments for refresh interval, retrace counter and
flags are all zero: owl draws via Quartz whenever Cocoa asks, with
no vsync coupling, hardware timestamps or zero-copy path, and no
way to predict the next refresh.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
